<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<graphml xmlns="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns/1.0/graphml.xsd">
  <key id="d11" for="edge" attr.name="type" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d10" for="edge" attr.name="seq" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d9" for="edge" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d8" for="edge" attr.name="flow_id" attr.type="long" />
  <key id="d7" for="node" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d6" for="node" attr.name="service" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d5" for="node" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d4" for="graph" attr.name="graph_usable" attr.type="boolean" />
  <key id="d3" for="graph" attr.name="categories" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d2" for="graph" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d1" for="graph" attr.name="link" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d0" for="graph" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <graph edgedefault="directed">
    <node id="0">
      <data key="d5">Smart Fire Hydrant</data>
      <data key="d6">UserConsumerIOT</data>
      <data key="d7">IoT smart fire hydrants that collect acoustic data measuring noises on the pipe network. Signals are recorded daily and correlated between multiple hydrants. Part of a large-scale IoT network of meters, hydrants, pressure monitoring and water quality devices.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1">
      <data key="d5">API Gateway</data>
      <data key="d6">ApiGateway</data>
      <data key="d7">Receives the acoustic files pushed daily from the devices. Provides authentication and security (heavy focus on IoT security), which is why data is not pushed directly to S3.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2">
      <data key="d5">S3</data>
      <data key="d6">S3</data>
      <data key="d7">Persists the acoustic signal files received through API Gateway.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3">
      <data key="d5">ECS</data>
      <data key="d6">ECS</data>
      <data key="d7">Daily batch process on container microservices. Processes each file as a node pair to look for correlations in the acoustic signals, including persistent correlations that occur over many days. Chosen over Lambda because the acoustic algorithms are very processor intensive.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4">
      <data key="d5">SageMaker</data>
      <data key="d6">SageMaker</data>
      <data key="d7">ML endpoint that classifies persistent correlations and assigns a leak probability (leak score). The model is retrained about every six weeks using feedback from the field, to reduce false positives.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5">
      <data key="d5">Analyst</data>
      <data key="d6">UserCompanyAnalyst</data>
      <data key="d7">Human analyst team that receives the leak score, reviews the persistent correlation, checks with the water utility for other nearby noise sources, and updates the leak status to 'field investigation required'.</data>
    </node>
    <edge source="0" target="1" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Fire hydrants push correlated acoustic signal files through API Gateway daily.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="1" target="2" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">After authentication and security at API Gateway, the files are pushed to and persisted in S3.</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="2" target="3" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">The daily ECS batch process processes each file from S3 as a node pair to look for correlations in the acoustic signals.</data>
      <data key="d10">2</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="3" target="4" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Persistent correlations found by ECS are sent to the SageMaker ML endpoint for classification and a leak probability.</data>
      <data key="d10">3</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4" target="5" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">The leak score from the SageMaker endpoint is fed to the human analyst team for further classification. Stated in the transcript; no arrow is drawn on the whiteboard.</data>
      <data key="d10">4</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <data key="d0">Mueller Water Products: Water Intelligence Platform - Acoustic Leak Detection</data>
    <data key="d1">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2e3vOxsHekE</data>
    <data key="d2">Smart fire hydrants record acoustic signals on the pipe network and push the files daily through API Gateway into S3. A daily ECS batch process finds correlations and persistent correlations, which a SageMaker ML endpoint classifies with a leak probability. Human analysts review the scores. The model is retrained about every six weeks using field feedback. Benefits: less non-revenue water loss and ecological protection.</data>
    <data key="d3">IoT, Machine Learning, Analytics</data>
    <data key="d4">True</data>
  </graph>
</graphml>
